22.1  SSH keys and the agent

TL;DR
  - A key pair replaces your password: the private key stays on your machine,
    the public one (.pub) goes into ~/.ssh/authorized_keys on each server.
  - Protect the private key with a passphrase, and let an agent remember it for
    the session, so you type it once.
  - Ed25519 is the type to create today: short, fast and safe.

      ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "alice@laptop"
      ssh-copy-id alice@server          # adds your public key over there
      ssh -v alice@server               # shows which keys are tried, and why

On your machine
  Keys      id_ed25519 (ssh-ed25519), id_rsa_old (ssh-rsa) open to others (644):
            ssh refuses it
  Config    3 host entries in ~/.ssh/config, with jump hosts
  Known     4 host keys trusted in known_hosts, names hashed
  Login     1 key may log in here as you (authorized_keys)
  Agent     none in this session: keys with a passphrase ask for it every time
  Server    port 2222 · password login no · root login prohibit-password
            (default)

Pitfalls
  - "Permissions 0644 for 'id_ed25519' are too open": ssh refuses a private key
    others can read. chmod 600 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519, and 700 on ~/.ssh.
  - "Permission denied (publickey)" with the right key: on the server, ~/.ssh or
    authorized_keys writable by others makes sshd ignore it.
  - Never copy the private key to a server to "jump" further: use ProxyJump
    (22.2).
  - Agent forwarding (ssh -A) lets anyone with root on that server use your keys
    while you're connected.

Deep dive

  ▾ How does a key log you in without being sent?

    The server picks a challenge built from the session; your client signs it
    with the private key; the server checks the signature with the public key it
    has in authorized_keys. Only someone holding the private key can produce
    that signature, and the signature is useless for any other session, so
    nothing reusable crosses the network.

  ▾ What does the agent do?

    ssh-agent keeps decrypted keys in memory and signs challenges for ssh, which
    reaches it through the socket named in SSH_AUTH_SOCK. Desktop sessions
    usually start one. AddKeysToAgent yes in ~/.ssh/config adds a key the first
    time you use it; ssh-add -l lists what's loaded.

Related
  Commands  ssh-keygen, ssh-copy-id, ssh-add, ssh
  Paths     ~/.ssh/, ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
  See also  idk 22.2 ~/.ssh/config, jump hosts and host keys
  See also  idk 9.1 rwx, octal and umask
